  "sections": [
    {
      "section_id": "medical_laryngoscope_section_1",
      "section_title": "Laryngoscope",
      "section_order": 1,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_1",
          "subsection_title": "Overview",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_1",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_01.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A laryngoscope is an instrument used to visualise the larynx and vocal cords, particularly during endotracheal intubation. In emergency medicine, anaesthesia, and neonatal resuscitation, it helps a trained clinician guide an endotracheal tube through the vocal cords into the trachea."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is a high-yield OSCE instrument because examiners often test identification of the handle and blade, differences between Macintosh and Miller blades, preparation, technique, and complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Direct laryngoscopy lifts the tongue and surrounding tissues to create a view of the laryngeal inlet. The laryngoscope itself does not ventilate the patient."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Name: Laryngoscope.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Main use: Visualisation of the larynx, especially for tracheal intubation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Common type in OSCEs: Direct laryngoscope.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Common blades: Macintosh (curved) and Miller (straight).",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Power source: Usually batteries or a rechargeable handle, although video laryngoscopes may have separate electronic components.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Types of laryngoscope",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Direct laryngoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses a light source and a blade to allow the operator to view the laryngeal structures directly. Commonly used in anaesthesia and emergency airway management."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Video laryngoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses a camera near the blade tip and displays the airway on a screen. It can improve the view in many situations but still requires training and correct tube-delivery technique."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Flexible endoscope"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A flexible endoscopic instrument that permits airway visualisation through the nose or mouth. It is used for selected diagnostic procedures and specialised airway management; it differs from a conventional direct laryngoscope."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Components and functions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Handle"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provides the grip and commonly contains the battery or power source."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Displaces the tongue and surrounding soft tissues to expose the laryngeal inlet."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Light source"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Illuminates the airway structures. Depending on the model, it may use a bulb or LED and may be located on the blade or handle assembly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Blade attachment and locking mechanism"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Connects the blade securely to the handle and activates the light on many conventional designs."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Macintosh versus Miller blades",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Macintosh blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Curved.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Tip is placed in the vallecula, the space between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Lifts the epiglottis indirectly by tension on the surrounding tissues.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Commonly used in adults and children.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Miller blade"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Straight.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The tip is used to lift the epiglottis directly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Often useful in neonatal and infant airway management, depending on the clinical situation and operator preference.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam memory tip: Macintosh = curved, vallecula, indirect lift. Miller = straight, direct epiglottis lift."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Neither blade is universally superior. Selection depends on patient anatomy, age, clinical circumstances, equipment, and operator skill."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Indications and uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "A laryngoscope may be used for:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Endotracheal intubation: Visualising the vocal cords to guide placement of an endotracheal tube.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Airway assessment: Evaluating visible laryngeal structures during selected airway procedures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Emergency airway management: In patients requiring a definitive airway, when indicated.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Anaesthesia: Facilitating tracheal intubation before or during general anaesthesia.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Neonatal resuscitation: Assisting tracheal intubation in selected newborns when indicated and performed by trained personnel.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Foreign-body assessment or removal: In selected cases, as part of specialist airway management.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides a view of the laryngeal inlet during intubation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Allows visual guidance of endotracheal tube placement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Conventional models are portable and reusable when designed for reprocessing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Different blades accommodate different patient anatomies.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Video laryngoscopes can provide an improved view and allow others to see the screen.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Disadvantages and limitations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Requires training and practice.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Direct visualisation may be difficult in patients with restricted mouth opening, airway swelling, secretions, blood, or abnormal anatomy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause dental, lip, tongue, or pharyngeal trauma.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Laryngoscopy may cause transient physiological responses, including tachycardia and hypertension.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Repeated or prolonged attempts can cause hypoxia and delay effective ventilation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A good view of the vocal cords does not guarantee successful tube passage or correct tube placement.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Preparation before use — OSCE checklist",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Before performing laryngoscopy, a trained clinician should:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Assess the airway and plan for anticipated difficulty.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Call for appropriate assistance.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check the laryngoscope handle, blade attachment, and light.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Select an appropriate blade size.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure the endotracheal tube and appropriate adjuncts are available.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check suction and oxygen delivery equipment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Prepare appropriate monitoring and a plan for confirming tube placement.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Preoxygenate the patient when indicated and feasible.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ensure a rescue ventilation and failed-airway plan is available.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: In an emergency, preparation should be rapid and systematic. Do not allow equipment checks to create unnecessary delays in lifesaving airway management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Direct laryngoscopy technique",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a procedural skill for trained clinicians. In an OSCE, demonstrate on a manikin unless you are specifically authorised and supervised to perform the procedure on a patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Position the patient: Use an appropriate airway position. The sniffing position is commonly used in adults when not contraindicated; positioning must be adapted for infants and other clinical circumstances.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Open the mouth: Use the appropriate hand and technique.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Insert the blade: Usually enter from the right side of the mouth, then displace the tongue towards the left.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Advance under vision: Identify the tongue and epiglottis rather than advancing blindly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Position the blade: With a Macintosh blade, place the tip in the vallecula. With a Miller blade, the tip is used to lift the epiglottis directly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Lift appropriately: Lift the handle forwards and upwards along the blade's axis to expose the laryngeal inlet. Do not lever against the teeth.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Identify the landmarks: Recognise the epiglottis, arytenoid region, and vocal cords as visible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Guide the tube: If intubation is indicated, the trained operator passes the endotracheal tube through the vocal cords under vision.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "8."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Confirm placement: Use waveform capnography where available and appropriate, together with clinical assessment. Visualisation during insertion alone is not sufficient confirmation.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "9."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reassess: Check ventilation, oxygenation, and the patient's response.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "10."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Contraindications and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Laryngoscopy may be lifesaving, so there is no simple absolute contraindication when emergency airway intervention is necessary. However, precautions include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected cervical spine injury: use appropriate spinal precautions and airway techniques.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Restricted mouth opening or significant facial trauma.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Airway swelling, bleeding, or copious secretions that impair visibility.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Anticipated difficult airway: seek experienced help and consider an alternative airway strategy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Physiological instability, including severe hypoxia or haemodynamic compromise.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Dental trauma",
                      "Excessive force or levering against the teeth"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Lip, tongue, or pharyngeal injury",
                      "Incorrect blade insertion or manipulation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Hypoxia",
                      "Prolonged attempts or inadequate oxygenation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "Regurgitated gastric contents entering the airway"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Haemodynamic response",
                      "Transient tachycardia or hypertension"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Airway swelling or bleeding",
                      "Tissue trauma during difficult or repeated attempts"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Failed intubation",
                      "Poor view, difficult anatomy, or inability to pass the tube"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Oesophageal intubation",
                      "Tube enters the oesophagus rather than the trachea"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Pediatric and neonatal considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_15.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/medical_laryngoscope_16.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use a blade designed for the child's or newborn's anatomy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A straight Miller blade is frequently used for neonatal intubation, although blade selection depends on the patient and operator.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Infants have a relatively large tongue, a more cephalad larynx, and a proportionally larger epiglottis than adults.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid excessive force and prolonged attempts.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain oxygenation and follow the relevant pediatric or neonatal airway protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use appropriate monitoring and confirm endotracheal tube placement with recommended methods.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Memory aids",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "MACINTOSH"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "M — Macintosh is curved.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A — Acts indirectly on the epiglottis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "C — Curved blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "I — Into the vallecula.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "N — No levering on teeth.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "T — Tongue displaced to the left.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "O — Observe the laryngeal landmarks.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "S — Select the appropriate size.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "H — Handle lifts forwards and upwards.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "MILLER"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "M — Mainly remembered as the straight blade.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "I — Its tip lifts the epiglottis directly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "L — Laryngeal exposure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "L — Lift without levering on teeth.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "E — Especially familiar in neonatal airway stations.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "R — Requires appropriate sizing and training.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "medical_laryngoscope_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. Common OSCE examiner questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "medical_laryngoscope_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is the function of a laryngoscope?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To visualise the laryngeal inlet, particularly during endotracheal intubation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What are the main parts?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The handle, blade, light source, and blade attachment mechanism."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between Macintosh and Miller blades?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The Macintosh blade is curved and usually positioned in the vallecula to lift the epiglottis indirectly. The Miller blade is straight and is used to lift the epiglottis directly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why should you avoid levering on the teeth?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can cause dental trauma and does not provide the correct lifting mechanics for laryngoscopy."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. How do you confirm successful endotracheal intubation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Waveform capnography is the preferred confirmation method where available. Clinical assessment and other appropriate checks support the assessment; chest rise alone is insufficient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What complications can occur?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Dental and airway trauma, hypoxia, aspiration, haemodynamic responses, and failed or misplaced intubation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why is a straight blade often used in neonates?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can help directly lift the relatively prominent epiglottis, although the choice depends on the clinical situation and operator expertise."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
